{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cbw2cTbCeBahiapw9NuKi5","type":"tool_result","content":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-challenger-twobz5zx\ntotal 16\ndrwx------@     4 brenn  staff     128 Oct  2 16:46 .\ndrwx------@ 13067 brenn  staff  418144 Oct  2 16:46 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x@    14 brenn  staff     448 Oct  2 12:21 agent\n-rw-r--r--@     1 brenn  staff    5866 Oct  2 16:46 sheet.md\n# Gap hunt: B03 — Credential lifetime and handling\n\nA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\ncheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\nlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n\nFor each check, decide:\n- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n  value never used, an exception that continues, a filter that lets something through).\n  Quote that line;\n- **holds**: you found the code that makes it true in every case;\n- **unclear**: you can't tell from this repo.\n\n**The requirement for B03:** Issue short-lived credentials from a managed identity or secrets service. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where the service supports it, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Then a copied token won't work from another machine. Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. Show that credentials expire and rotate, and explain why you chose their lifetime.\n\nIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n\n\n## B03.1\n\n**Check:** Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** IRSA and Secrets Manager used; payments and SMS keys are static provider keys and short-lived alternatives are unknown\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## B03.2\n\n**Check:** Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** IRSA 3600s, projected token 900s, EHR token 5 minutes set; no rotation resource for stored secrets in terraform, may be managed outside\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## B03.3\n\n**Check:** No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an image, or placed in a prompt; `.gitignore` listing a file does not count if it is committed.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:** Dockerfile:13 — \"CLINIC_AGENT_ROOT=/app\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** No long-lived secrets in image, config, or prompts\n\n`Dockerfile` lines 1–22 (cited lines marked >):\n```\n   1  # syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7\n   2  FROM node:22.11-bookworm-slim@sha256:3f1a8d2c9b7e4f6a5d0c8b1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b7c8d9e0f1a AS build\n   3  WORKDIR /app\n   4  COPY package.json package-lock.json ./\n   5  RUN npm ci --ignore-scripts\n   6  COPY tsconfig.json ./\n   7  COPY src ./src\n   8  RUN npm run build && npm prune --omit=dev\n   9  \n  10  FROM gcr.io/distroless/nodejs22-debian12:nonroot@sha256:9b2e4c6a8d0f1e3c5a7b9d1f3e5c7a9b1d3f5e7c9a1b3d5f7e9c1a3b5d7f9e1c\n  11  WORKDIR /app\n  12  ENV NODE_ENV=production \\\n  13>     CLINIC_AGENT_ROOT=/app\n  14  COPY --from=build --chown=65532:65532 /app/node_modules ./node_modules\n  15  COPY --from=build --chown=65532:65532 /app/dist ./dist\n  16  COPY --chown=65532:65532 package.json ./\n  17  COPY --chown=65532:65532 prompts ./prompts\n  18  COPY --chown=65532:65532 config ./config\n  19  COPY --chown=65532:65532 release ./release\n  20  USER 65532:65532\n  21  EXPOSE 8080\n  22  CMD [\"dist/server.js\"]\n```\n\n## B03.4\n\n**Check:** Where the service supports it, tokens are bound to their holder with mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find token and client setup for each service (`mTLS`, `client_cert`, `DPoP`, `cert-bound`, SPIFFE). 2) Check whether the service supports holder binding. 3) Answer no if a supporting service uses plain bearer tokens. Answer unknown if support depends on a provider outside this repo and nothing states it.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Bearer tokens used; only server certificate pinned for EHR, no client-cert binding; provider support unknown\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## Your answer\n\nWrite this JSON to B03.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"B03\"):\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"B03.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\n \"note\": \"one sentence\"}\